course: space
lesson: 12
title: Saturn and its rings
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Saturn is a giant ball of gas, mostly hydrogen and helium, about nine times wider than the Earth, and so light for its size that it would float in an impossibly huge bath.
explain: Its famous rings are not solid plates but countless chunks of water ice, from tiny grains to pieces as big as a house, each going round Saturn like a tiny moon.
explain: Picture a sheet of paper as wide as a football pitch: Saturn's rings are even flatter than that, about 280,000 kilometres across but usually only about 10 metres thick.
explain: That is why, when the rings turn edge-on to us every 13 to 16 years, they almost vanish, as they did in March 2025, like a sheet of paper seen from the side.
explain: The rings have gaps swept clear by the pull of Saturn's moons, and they slowly lose ice that rains into the planet, so scientists still argue about how old they are.
fact: Saturn is the second largest planet, about nine times wider than the Earth.
fact: Like Jupiter, Saturn is made mostly of hydrogen and helium.
fact: Saturn is the least dense planet, less dense than water, so in an impossibly huge bath it would float.
fact: Saturn's rings are made of countless chunks of water ice, from tiny grains to pieces as big as a house, and the ice makes them shine brightly, as fresh snow does; dark rock would look much dimmer.
fact: Saturn's main rings stretch about 280,000 kilometres across but are usually only about 10 metres thick.
fact: The rings have gaps, the biggest called the Cassini Division, cleared by the gravity of Saturn's moons.
fact: Every 13 to 16 years Saturn's rings line up edge-on to the Earth and almost disappear from view, which last happened in March 2025.
fact: Saturn's rings are slowly losing material that falls into the planet.
fact: Scientists still argue about whether Saturn's rings are young or as old as Saturn itself.
fact: Saturn's north pole has a six-sided jet stream called the hexagon, each side wider than the Earth.
fact: Saturn takes about 29.5 Earth years to orbit the Sun, and its winds reach about 1,800 kilometres per hour, many times faster than the strongest hurricanes on Earth, which reach about 300.
fact: The Cassini spacecraft orbited Saturn from 2004 to 2017 and finished by diving into Saturn on purpose.
fact: All four giant planets have rings, but Saturn's are by far the biggest and brightest.
exercise: Where does Saturn come in size among the planets? | Second largest, after Jupiter | The largest of all | Third largest, after Jupiter and Neptune | The smallest of the giants
exercise: About how many times wider than the Earth is Saturn? | About nine times | About twice | About a hundred times | About the same width
exercise: What is Saturn mostly made of? | Hydrogen and helium | Rock and iron, like Earth | Liquid water and ice | Ice, dust and frozen gas
exercise: Why would Saturn float in an impossibly huge bath? | It is less dense than water | It is filled with air like a balloon | Its rings hold it up like a float | It is hollow in the middle
exercise: What are Saturn's rings made of? | Countless chunks of water ice | One solid, shining sheet of rock | Glowing coloured gas, like neon | Bands of hot, runny liquid metal
exercise: How big are the pieces in Saturn's rings? | From tiny grains to pieces as big as a house | All exactly the size of a football | Each one as big as a mountain range | Too small to see even under a microscope
exercise: About how thick are Saturn's main rings usually? | About 10 metres | About 10,000 kilometres | About as thick as the Earth | About 280,000 kilometres
exercise: What is the Cassini Division? | The biggest gap in Saturn's rings | The largest moon of Saturn | The storm at Saturn's north pole | The line where Saturn's day starts
exercise: What clears the gaps in Saturn's rings? | The gravity of Saturn's moons | Winds blowing out from Saturn | Spacecraft flying through them | Comets crashing through the ice
exercise: Why do Saturn's rings almost vanish every 13 to 16 years? | They line up edge-on to the Earth | They melt in Saturn's summer | Saturn's moons eat them up | They fall into Saturn and grow back
exercise: When did Saturn's rings last turn edge-on to the Earth? | In March 2025 | In 1610 | In 2004 | They never turn edge-on
exercise: What is slowly happening to Saturn's rings? | They lose material that falls into Saturn | They grow by a new ring every year | They drift off towards Jupiter | They are turning into a new moon each century
exercise: What do scientists still argue about? | Whether Saturn's rings are young or as old as Saturn | Whether Saturn has any rings at all | Whether Saturn goes round the Sun | Whether Saturn's rings are made of ice
exercise: What is Saturn's hexagon? | A six-sided jet stream at its north pole | A six-sided crater on the surface of one of its moons | A six-sided gap in its rings | A star pattern seen behind Saturn
exercise: How long does Saturn take to orbit the Sun? | About 29.5 Earth years | About 1 Earth year, like us | About 165 Earth years, like Neptune | About 12 Earth days only
exercise: How did the Cassini spacecraft end its mission in 2017? | It dived into Saturn on purpose | It landed gently on the rings | It flew off out of the Solar System | It came back to the Earth
exercise: Which giant planets have rings? | All four: Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune | Only Saturn | Only Saturn and Jupiter, the two biggest giants of all | None except Saturn and Uranus
test: My little brother thinks Saturn's rings are like a hard plastic hoop you could walk on. What is the truth? | They are separate chunks of ice each orbiting Saturn | They are one solid ring of rock | They are a hoop of frozen gas you could skate on | They are just light, with nothing there at all
test: A cartoon shows Saturn sinking like a stone when it is dropped into a giant bath. What should happen? | It should float, as it is less dense than water | It should sink fast, as it is so heavy | It should melt away in the water | It should explode on touching water
test: my dad looked through his telescope in 2025 and said Saturn had lost its rings. bloody hell, have they gone?? | No, they were edge-on to us and too thin to see | Yes, they fell into Saturn that year | Yes, a comet smashed them to bits | No, a moon was blocking the view
test: If the rings were made of rock instead of ice, how would they look? | Much darker and less bright | Much brighter and shinier than now | Exactly the same as they look now | They would be completely invisible
test: Why could you not fly a spacecraft down onto Saturn and park it? | Saturn is gas, with no solid surface | Saturn's surface is too slippery | Saturn's rings stop anything getting near | Saturn spins too slowly to land on
test: Mia says the rings will be there for ever, exactly the same. what's wrong with that? | They slowly lose ice that falls into Saturn | They can't change at all, because they are one solid piece | Nothing, rings never change at all | They are getting bigger every year
test: A child born when Saturn was at one point of its orbit is how old when Saturn first gets back there? | About 29 or 30 | About 1 | About 12 | About 84, like Uranus
test: Which is true? | Saturn has the biggest and brightest rings, but other giants have rings too | Only Saturn has rings | Saturn's rings are thicker than the Earth | Saturn is the biggest planet
test: why's the Cassini Division there, ain't the rings meant to be full | The pull of Saturn's moons keeps it clear | A spaceship cut a hole there | The Sun melted the ice there | It is the shadow of Saturn, not a gap
test: Which of these would fit inside one side of Saturn's hexagon? | The whole Earth | Nothing, the sides are tiny | Only a small moon | Only a city
test: Saturn's winds reach about 1,800 kilometres per hour. Compared with the strongest hurricanes on Earth, they are | Many times faster | About the same | Much slower | Exactly twice as slow
exam: Identify the principal constituent of the material composing the rings of Saturn. | Water ice | Molten iron | Incandescent gas | Solid granite
exam: State the reason for which the planet Saturn would, in principle, float upon water. | Its mean density is lower than that of water | It is hollow and contains air | It is supported by its rings | It is composed chiefly of wood-like matter
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the thickness of Saturn's main rings. | It is typically in the order of ten metres | It is in excess of ten thousand kilometres | It exceeds the diameter of the Earth | It is equal to their breadth
exam: Ascertain the manner in which the Cassini mission was concluded in the year two thousand and seventeen. | By a deliberate descent into Saturn | By a landing upon the rings | By a return to the Earth | By departure from the Solar System altogether
